                  "toolName": "WebSearch",
                  "output": "Web search results for query: \"Queensland Electric Super Highway Yurika chargers app pay how to charge\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Welcome to the Queensland Electric Super Highway\",\"url\":\"https://www.yurika.com.au/capability/qld-electric-super-highway/\"},{\"title\":\"Yurika EV Charging Network\",\"url\":\"https://evlog.net.au/yurika-ev-charging-network/\"},{\"title\":\"Queensland Electric Super Highway (QESH) - Yurika\",\"url\":\"https://www.yurika.com.au/case-study/queensland-electric-super-highway-qesh/\"},{\"title\":\"EV Charging & Roadside Assistance in Brisbane\",\"url\":\"https://evroadside.com.au/locations/brisbane/\"},{\"title\":\"Electric car drivers can now use Chargefox network from Adelaide to Cairns\",\"url\":\"https://thedriven.io/2019/07/02/electric-car-drivers-can-now-use-chargefox-network-from-adelaide-to-cairns/\"},{\"title\":\"Queensland identifies two more sites for $2.5million EV network rollout\",\"url\":\"https://thedriven.io/2020/02/17/queensland-identifies-two-more-sites-for-2-5million-ev-network-rollout/\"},{\"title\":\"Third phase of EV Super Highway to add 23 new charging stations\",\"url\":\"https://thedriven.io/2024/01/15/third-phase-of-ev-super-highway-to-add-23-new-charging-stations/\"},{\"title\":\"Chargefox extends EV network to the Queensland super highway\",\"url\":\"https://autotalk.com.au/industry-news/chargefox-extends-ev-network-queensland-super-highway\"},{\"title\":\"Queensland’s electric super highway to expand to over 30 electric car chargers\",\"url\":\"https://thedriven.io/2019/05/31/queensland-s-electric-super-highway-to-expand-to-over-50-electric-car-chargers/\"},{\"title\":\"Electric dreaming: Discover the 109 EV charging stations in Queensland - Flare\",\"url\":\"https://www.flarehr.com/blog/exploring-ev-charging-stations-in-queensland\"}]\n\nTo charge on the Queensland Electric Super Highway (QESH), you use the Chargefox app. The network is run by Yurika, but Chargefox handles payment.\n\n**How to charge**\n- Yurika's official page says to get charging you'll need the Chargefox App, which you can download for iPhone or Android. You'll need to create an account before you plug in and start charging. The app shows how your charge is going in real time, so you don't need to keep checking back.\n- A Chargefox RFID card also works. An EV directory lists activation by the Chargefox app or RFID, with no Autocharge.\n- Connectors: DC charging uses CHAdeMO and CCS Combo Type 2 cables. AC charging uses a Type 2 Mennekes socket, and you bring your own cable. Each DC charger has a backup AC charger, which also needs your own Type 2 cable.\n- Help: visit www.chargefox.com or call 1300 518 038.\n\n**Cost**\n- Pricing varies by location. As a general guideline, slower chargers usually cost less and faster chargers usually cost more. Current prices and locations are in the Chargefox app. None of the results gave a current per-kWh rate. The last figure I found was from January 2020, when a 20c/kWh fee was introduced, so it's likely out of date.\n\n**Background and trip planning**\n- The QESH is a Queensland Government initiative, built and operated by Yurika and supported by RACQ. It has over 50 charging stations from Coolangatta to Port Douglas, extending into regional Queensland.\n- For route planning, Yurika recommends the PlugShare Trip Planner, which also shows whether stations are currently working.\n\nSources: yurika.com.au/capability/qld-electric-super-highway/ (updated about a month ago) and evlog.net.au.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.",
